2026-08 · Dependency doctrine: nothing revocable in the critical path. Test is not "open source" but (1) permissive licence for embedded code, (2) governance that isn't one company, (3) standard protocol so the vendor is a config line. Evidence: MinIO was AGPL open source and still archived/stripped (Feb–Apr 2026) — vendor-as-commons is the risk, not licence text.
2026-08 · Class A eliminated: multi-account OCI (ToS + isolated tenancies + termination risk), MinIO (use pgBackRest→any S3-compatible; ≥2 targets, one off-provider), hotlinked Google Fonts (privacy/GDPR — removed from our own mockup), CDN-loaded JS (bundle + CSP), Docker Desktop (use Engine/Podman).
2026-08 · Class B swaps behind interfaces: Turnstile→ALTCHA (MIT, self-hosted, Argon2id PoW); Cloudflare Tunnel→WireGuard+CrowdSec if needed; GitHub→Forgejo mirror from day one; Grafana/Loki(AGPL)→VictoriaMetrics/Logs(Apache-2.0) if licence drifts; R2→any S3-compatible or self-hosted Garage/SeaweedFS.
2026-08 · Class C accepted as irreducible: a physical host, OTA marketplaces, payment rails, government endpoints, UAE ASP, browser push services, public CA, volumetric DDoS absorption. Portability (Compose+Kamal, zero cloud-specific services) is the protection, not avoidance.
2026-08 · We code ourselves: outbox event bus, auth (JWT+argon2id+TOTP), rate limiting, fiscal/statutory adapters, backup+restore drills, direct channel adapters. We never build: crypto, databases, TLS, solvers, compression, font rendering.
2026-08 · Phase-0 CI gates added: license-check allowlist {MIT,Apache-2.0,BSD,ISC,PostgreSQL,MPL-2.0} with documented exceptions; CSP test asserting zero third-party origins; AGPL tools permitted only standalone/unmodified/internal.
2026-08 · Field prototype reviewed (working PMS, localhost:5173). Adopted: travel_detail (mode/carrier/service_no/ETA/pickup → automation raises transfer task), vehicle register (plate-searchable; security register is standard at gated IN/Gulf properties), folio_no human-readable reference (confirmation_no already existed), property switcher, Reports screen incl. Security/Vehicles, property setup workbench with bulk room create (range+prefix+zero-pad OR pasted list). Schema now 80 tables; RLS auto-applied; fixture + 11/11 battery re-validated.
2026-08 · Parking slots are NOT a new primitive: a slot is a space with profile_key='parking', assigned through record_occupancy like any bedroom. Vehicle table carries registration details only.
2026-08 · Rejected from prototype: modal reservation dialog (no URL → not deep-linkable, not restorable on refresh, forces modal stacking). Keep DRAWER at /p/{property}/res/{id}.
2026-08 · Terminology familiarity: hotel vertical may LABEL the day-close surface "Night Audit" via vertical_profile.terminology while the engine remains continuous close. Adopt OPERA's vocabulary, never its architecture.
2026-08 · Tooling set deliberately small: postgres + github + context7 in .mcp.json. Every MCP server costs context budget on every session and every credential widens the trust surface. Playwright added at Phase 10, error-tracking MCP once GlitchTip runs; nothing speculative.
2026-08 · Not installed: Filesystem MCP (Claude Code ships file tools with sandboxing — redundant), web-search MCP (built in; Exa stays in claude.ai for research).
2026-08 · Fixed config bug found during wiring: .mcp.json postgres DSN used user 'pms_app' while docker-compose creates 'pms' — would have failed to connect on session one. Now postgresql://yellow:yellow@127.0.0.1:5442/yellow_dev.
2026-08 · Marketplace policy (docs/TOOLING.md): ~200 community marketplaces and thousands of plugins, largely unvetted; skills/hooks/MCP servers execute locally with GitHub token + DB credentials. Rule = read the code, permissive licence + real maintainer, recent activity, and "what does it replace?". Prefer Anthropic's official directory or first-party vendor servers. Cost-observability CLIs recommended but NOT auto-installed by setup.sh.
2026-08 · Project codename: YELLOW. Fully isolated from any other project on the machine — containers yellow-postgres/yellow-valkey, ports 5442/6389 (not 5432/6379, which an existing PMS may hold), databases yellow_dev/yellow_test, role yellow, GitHub repo 'yellow', and skills renamed yellow-* so they cannot collide with another project's skills in ~/.claude/skills/. Domain vocabulary (PMS, folio, space) unchanged — Yellow is the project name, not the product category.
2026-08 · Windows 11 development uses WSL2 + Ubuntu 24.04, not native Windows. Reasons: the stack is Linux-native (Docker Postgres, bash tooling), the MCP ecosystem targets macOS/Linux (native Windows hits spawn npx ENOENT and path-quoting issues), Claude Code sandboxed execution works on WSL2 but not native Windows, and it keeps both founders' machines running identical commands. Project must live in the Linux filesystem (~/projects), never /mnt/c — 9P boundary kills I/O and file watching. .gitattributes forces LF endings to prevent CRLF breaking bash scripts.
2026-08 · Editor stack: NOT VS Code. Windows Terminal (split panes) as primary surface + Zed as GUI editor (Rust/DirectX, non-Electron, native WSL integration, 1.0 Apr 2026) + lazygit for diff review + optional helix for terminal edits. Rationale: in an agentic workflow Claude Code writes and the human reads/reviews/approves, so optimise for reading and diffing rather than IDE features. Zed's ~800-extension ecosystem is irrelevant here (TS/SQL/Markdown are first-class; DB browsing goes through psql + postgres MCP).
2026-08 · Bug found on first real run (Windows/WSL2): the yellow rename missed the keyword-form DSN in setup.sh (user=pms password=pms port=5432) — URL-form and psql -U forms were covered, this one was not. Test runner knocked on 5432 with the wrong user while Postgres listened on 5442. Fixed; runner fallback DSN now also matches docker-compose. Lesson: renames must cover EVERY connection-string dialect (URL, keyword, CLI flags), and a config rename needs an end-to-end run to verify, not a grep.
2026-08 · Runner hardened: connection failures inside worker threads now count as a failed claim instead of raising, so a dead database prints clean FAIL lines rather than ~50 stack traces.
2026-08 · Dual-agent support added: OpenAI Codex CLI can run against Yellow alongside Claude Code, since the project itself (schema, docker-compose, tests, docs) has zero agent-specific lock-in — consistent with DEPENDENCIES.md's "nothing revocable" doctrine applied to tooling. Bridged: AGENTS.md (trimmed mirror of CLAUDE.md, model-name-agnostic), .codex/config.toml (mirrors .mcp.json's three servers). DECISIONS.log is explicitly SHARED between both agents — check it before deciding, not after, to avoid re-litigating settled questions across tools. Suggested split: Codex for well-specified mechanical work (cheap/free), Claude Fable/Opus for the ambiguous 20% (schema, occupancy, ledger, fiscal, RLS) — same escalation principle as the existing model policy, spread across tools instead of models. Not bridged: Claude Code's auto-loading skills mechanism and PostToolUse hooks have no Codex equivalent yet; skills must be referenced explicitly until/unless Codex ships a comparable loader.
2026-08 · Dual-agent division of labour: Codex = builder (volume implementation from work orders), Claude Fable 5 = architect/reviewer (writes orders, reviews diffs, decides invariant questions, sole appender of DECISIONS.log). Rationale: spend the expensive agent only where ambiguity lives; nine research rounds removed ambiguity from most execution work. Handoff is FILE-BASED (handoff/orders, /reviews, /questions, LEDGER.md) because neither agent sees the other's sessions — the files are the shared memory and they version with the code.
2026-08 · Git conventions for two agents: main only via reviewed PR; branches phase-N/slug; commit prefixes [codex]/[claude] so attribution survives in git log; .gitattributes gives DECISIONS.log and handoff/LEDGER.md union merge so parallel appends don't conflict; ./setup.sh --db-only (11/11) is required BEFORE a PR is reviewable, not after.
2026-08 · Merge with existing PMS: Yellow's schema is the base, the existing system is the requirements/UX source — one-way flow. Reason: a working screen can be ported onto a correct foundation, but tenant isolation, claim-range occupancy, and an insert-only ledger cannot be retrofitted into a system whose data already flows otherwise. Merge starts no earlier than Phase 5 (nothing to migrate into before the ledger exists). Capture legacy schema dump, screenshots, feature inventory, usage notes, and sample data into docs/legacy/ NOW while memory is fresh. Never port legacy code directly — port the requirements it embodies.
2026-08 · Single-source-of-truth refactor for multi-agent scale: PROJECT.md is now the ONE canonical constitution (Ten Invariants, boundaries, standards, never-do, session ritual, where-truth-lives map). CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md reduced to thin adapters = pointer + role only. Rationale: duplicated rules drift; two copies survive, four don't. Explicit precedence rule: if an adapter contradicts PROJECT.md, PROJECT.md wins and the contradiction is a bug.
2026-08 · state.sh added: read-only script printing identical ground truth for every agent (branch/head/dirty, last ledger entry, open orders+questions, last 5 decisions, container status, table count, reading order). "On the same page" must be verifiable, not assumed — every session starts here.
2026-08 · Review tiers (handoff/ROSTER.md), a property of the CHANGE not the author: T1 routine = one architect approval + green battery; T2 invariant-adjacent = architect approval + a test that would fail if the invariant broke; T3 foundational (migrations, occupancy, ledger, fiscal, RLS, doc numbering) = TWO reviewers FROM DIFFERENT VENDORS + executable proof. Cross-vendor requirement is evidence-based, not ceremony: the view-RLS leak was missed by two independent paper reviews (models share blind spots and can agree for the same wrong reason) and caught only by a fixture that ran.
2026-08 · Adding an agent is a config entry, not a redesign: create <VENDOR>.md as pointer+role (never copy invariants), add a ROSTER row with tier and cost posture, mirror MCP config to that tool's dialect, claim a commit prefix, shake down on a Tier-1 order, log the addition. Permanent rules for all agents: nobody merges their own work; DECISIONS.log is shared/append-only/union-merged; commit prefixes mandatory; agent disagreement is resolved by writing a test that settles it, and if none can, it's a founder decision recorded with both positions.
2026-08 · Audit of the multi-agent refactor found real drift: all six onboarding paths (README, START-HERE, START-HERE-WINDOWS, USAGE, setup.sh, WALKTHROUGH html) still instructed "Read CLAUDE.md and BUILD-PLAN.md" — written before CLAUDE.md became a 34-line adapter. Anyone following a guide as written would never reach PROJECT.md, never run state.sh, never discover handoff/. Fixed: handoff line now "Read PROJECT.md, then CLAUDE.md and BUILD-PLAN.md"; state.sh surfaced as the first daily step in USAGE/START-HERE/walkthrough/setup output; file tables list PROJECT.md, state.sh, AGENTS.md, handoff/, WORKFLOW, CODEX, MERGE-PLAN. Lesson: a refactor is not done when the new files exist — it is done when every path INTO the project points at them.
2026-08 · Final package consolidation: ARCHITECTURE-v3.html and the four research rounds (differential-analysis 1-3, system-stress-test-4, master build prompt) folded into docs/ and docs/research/. Reason: DECISIONS.log records WHAT was decided; the research archive records WHY, which is what any future agent or founder needs when asking "can we just change X?" — without it, a locked decision looks arbitrary and gets relitigated.
2026-08-14 · D-63 · Orders 001-006 were authored AND implemented by Codex ("temporary architect by founder request"). Ratified retroactively: the founder authorized it, Codex labelled it in every order file, stopped at the Tier-3 line, and wrote handoff/questions/007.md instead of implementing through it. But the split in docs/WORKFLOW.md exists precisely because a self-authored order cannot catch its own blind spot, and review findings F1 (headers applied at the request edge, tested only on /health) and F2 (unpinned compiler bypassing the licence gate the same agent built) are that blind spot made concrete. Standing rule from Order 007 onward: orders come from an architect-role agent; if none is available the builder writes handoff/questions/NNN.md and WAITS. Rejected: voiding orders 001-006 and rebuilding (the work is sound; the process gap is now closed and the defects are being fixed by Order 007).
2026-08-14 · D-64 · Licence exceptions are NOT file-driven. docs/DEPENDENCIES.md promised a docs/licence-exceptions.md bypass; that file never existed and Order 004 forbade creating it. Doctrine now: an exception requires an architect decision in DECISIONS.log plus an approved order. Rejected: implementing the exceptions file as documented — a gate a builder can unblock by writing a file in its own scope is not a gate, and the whole point of the licence check is that it cannot be self-cleared. docs/DEPENDENCIES.md "Enforcement" corrected by Order 007.
2026-08-14 · D-65 · SPDX OR is a choice, not a conjunction: an expression passes when at least one operand subtree is fully allowed (MIT OR GPL-3.0 passes, we take MIT); AND still requires all operands; the deprecated `licenses` array is OR semantics per npm's historic meaning; the legacy singular object form {"type":"MIT"} is accepted. The checker MUST print which operand it accepted, so the effective licence is visible in the CI log rather than implicit. Everything else stays fail-closed (WITH, LicenseRef-*, + suffixes, unknown, absent, malformed). Rejected: keeping strict all-operands evaluation — it produces false positives on legitimately permissive dual-licensed packages, and the first time one fires the pressure will be to add a blanket exception, which is exactly the bypass D-64 closes. Fixed now while the tree is 21 packages and the change costs one test line.
2026-08-14 · D-66 · `bun audit` stays blocking and unsuppressed even though an upstream advisory can turn a previously-green tree red with no change on our side. That is the gate working, not flaking. A new advisory in a pinned dependency is a STOP-and-ask (handoff/questions/NNN.md), never a suppression or an unpin. Rejected: non-blocking/advisory-only audit, and severity thresholds — both convert a signal into a log line nobody reads.
2026-08-14 · D-67 · The 13 bounded contexts are fixed by migrations/0001_init.sql's own section headers, which number Contexts 1-13 explicitly: 1 identity, 2 inventory, 3 rates, 4 reservations, 5 stay-operations, 6 housekeeping, 7 financials, 8 crm, 9 groups, 10 distribution, 11 tax-fiscal, 12 statutory-privacy, 13 reporting. This resolves the "12 vs 13" ambiguity in handoff/questions/007.md: the research catalogue's 12 folds Contexts 11 and 12 together exactly as schema §10 does in one SQL section — the context numbering is authoritative, the section grouping is file-layout convenience, and they were never in conflict. Schema §2 KERNEL PRIMITIVES (extension registry, fact_log, outbox, document, task) carries NO context number and is therefore platform, not a context: it lives at src/kernel/, is never a 14th context, may be imported by any context, and may import none — the acyclic rule is what stops the kernel becoming a junk drawer. Contexts 5/6 ordering is cosmetic with no behavioural consequence; fixed as 5=stay-operations, 6=housekeeping so it stops being re-litigated. Rejected: deciding slugs from the research catalogue or from the adapter docs — the executable schema is the only artifact that cannot drift from what actually runs.
2026-08-14 · D-68 · Forgejo mirror and Cloudflare Tunnel are removed from the Phase 0 repository DoD. Forgejo = required before first deployment (insurance against GitHub is worth little while nothing is deployed to lose); Cloudflare Tunnel = deferred until the OCI hosts exist (there is no host to tunnel to). Both are FOUNDER actions requiring account creation and credentials — no agent creates accounts, exposes ports, or invents target infrastructure. Consequence stated plainly so it is not re-argued: Phase 0 can be marked complete without either, because the Phase 0 DoD is the repository proving the loop and neither is in that loop. Rejected: blocking Phase 0 completion on external infrastructure that no agent is authorized to create.
2026-08-14 · D-69 · tests/run_invariants.py is ARCHITECT-ONLY, same tier as migrations/. Raised by review finding F6: commit bbfe607 ("add Windows setup and verification support") removed `SET ROLE postgres` from TC-12.3's dorm-clearing setup, on a branch with no order, no Scope list and no DoD. The change is not a Windows fix and the battery stayed green, which is exactly why nobody saw it — the DELETE now runs as `yellow`, which owns the schema and bypasses RLS today, but RLS filters rather than raises, so the day `space_occupancy` gets FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY the setup will silently delete zero rows and TC-12.3 will fail for a reason unrelated to its cause. Doctrine: the battery is the instrument that grades every phase, and the agent being graded must not be able to adjust it. Any change to tests/run_invariants.py now requires an order that states, per affected TC, what the change does to that test's guarantees. Rejected: reverting bbfe607's line and restoring `SET ROLE postgres` — it is what broke portably (the role is absent on the founder's Docker setup); the correct fix is to assert the postcondition after the DELETE so a failed precondition fails loudly instead of silently. Rejected: treating this as benign because 11/11 stayed green — a green result from a weakened instrument is the failure mode the rule exists to prevent.
2026-08-14 · D-70 · DECISIONS.log is now tracked in git. Review finding F7: .gitignore line 5 (`*.log`) silently excluded it, so the project's canonical decision record — 69 entries at the moment of discovery — had never been under version control on any branch since the repository was created. .gitattributes:13 already declares `DECISIONS.log merge=union`, a merge strategy for a file git was not tracking; that contradiction is the proof the exclusion was accidental rather than intended. Consequences while it lasted: the file existed only in whichever working tree last wrote it (it survived this session only because it was hand-copied from the Windows tree into ~/projects/yellow); Codex and Claude could not in fact share it, despite D-53 declaring it SHARED between both agents and handoff/README.md promising these files "are versioned with the code"; and ./state.sh reported a decision count from the working tree that no fresh clone could reproduce. Fix: negation entry `!DECISIONS.log` in .gitignore. Rejected: narrowing the `*.log` glob to specific paths — the negation is one line and states the exception exactly where a reader looking for it will be, while leaving genuine log output ignored. Rejected: leaving it untracked and treating the working copy as canonical — that makes the decision record the single least durable artifact in a repository whose entire handoff model assumes the opposite.
2026-08-15 · D-71 · Founder-authorized temporary architect exception: while Claude is unavailable until 2026-08-16, OpenAI Codex may answer Question 008, append explicitly temporary decisions, and write Phase-0 orders. Attribution remains `[codex]` and every artifact says who wrote it; Codex does not impersonate Claude, approve its own later implementation, or merge. Claude must ratify or amend these artifacts before the cumulative Phase-0 integration PR merges. Rejected: remaining idle despite explicit founder direction; also rejected: relabelling Codex output `[claude]` or treating founder authorization as independent technical review.
2026-08-15 · D-72 · The architect-only invariant battery is hardened at two harness preconditions without weakening domain predicates. TC-12.3 must prove its cleanup connection is not subject to RLS (`row_security_active(...) = false`) and has DELETE privilege before deleting, then verify zero remaining dorm rows through that proven-unfiltered connection; restoring literal `SET ROLE postgres` stays forbidden. TC-12.5 uses a monotonic high-resolution clock and requires positive elapsed time before reporting throughput. The current Compose role `yellow` is superuser+BYPASSRLS, so D-69's governance finding stands but its claim that FORCE RLS would filter this exact role is corrected: superusers/BYPASSRLS always bypass. Rejected: a count-only postcondition through an unproven role, because RLS can make both DELETE and SELECT observe zero while rows remain.
2026-08-15 · D-73 · Phase-0 migrations use raw Bun SQL on one reserved connection, one session-level advisory lock for the whole run (signed bigint key 6441674055002974567, the eight-byte ASCII mnemonic `YelloMig`), and one transaction per numbered file. Files match `^[0-9]{4}_[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]*\.sql$`; numeric gaps are allowed, but duplicate versions, malformed names, applied files missing locally, filename disagreement, and exact-byte SHA-256 mismatch hard-fail. Baseline 0001 is additionally pinned to SHA-256 `fe2a9fc949c6bacded3f8d3fc4d14fc596a83ebde9aeb043eb10845f07b30923` and remains byte-for-byte immutable. The runner bootstraps `public.schema_migration(version bigint PK, filename text UNIQUE, checksum_sha256 char(64), applied_at timestamptz)` as deploy-owned non-tenant metadata, revokes PUBLIC and app_role access after every file, and never uses app_role for migrations. PostgreSQL is the parser: transaction-incompatible SQL must fail and roll back with no ledger row; Phase 0 has no nontransactional escape mode. Rejected: transaction-level locks between files, whole-set atomicity, environment-derived lock keys, checksum overrides, brittle SQL keyword parsing, and requiring contiguous version numbers.
2026-08-15 · D-74 · The Phase-0 bootstrap seed is an explicit local/CI demo seed, not production onboarding: tenant `yellow-demo` / `Yellow Demo` (UUIDv5 `6d9b7ce2-2d14-5576-b8c3-80f06501a603`) and property `yellow_demo.property` / `Yellow Demo Property` (UUIDv5 `4518a22f-b455-54c6-a50a-4584383749b9`), timezone UTC, currency USD. UUIDv5 is computed in TypeScript from the standard URL namespace and fixed names, verified by the RFC vector, because immutable 0001 does not install uuid-ossp. A deploy connection opens one transaction, `SET LOCAL ROLE app_role`, inserts the global tenant row, sets transaction-local `app.tenant_id`, then inserts the property. Identical reruns are no-ops; any same deterministic ID/slug/path with different canonical values hard-fails instead of updating or hiding drift. The two-tenant isolation fixture remains separate. Rejected: random/manual UUIDs, adding uuid-ossp to 0001, owner-bypass seeding, blind ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, and putting demo data in numbered migrations.
2026-08-15 · D-75 · Phase-0 database CI uses only the pinned Compose PostgreSQL service (`postgres:16.15-alpine@sha256:ab5c955e9e57ae9879d4411ab49a912be9d162455676f7bf56e951b11ac73785`) so local and CI preload/extension configuration cannot drift. It proves two separate contracts: deployment acceptance on an empty DB through the production runner+demo seed+health/exact-data checks, and invariant acceptance on a separate DB through the same runner+architect fixture+the existing Python 11-test referee. The Python battery remains the sole domain/RLS oracle and is strengthened in TC-13.1/13.4 with catalog assertions for tenant-table RLS/policies and every public view's security_invoker option; view behavior must return the expected tenant identity, not merely one distinct tenant. Schema drift compares a normalized pinned-PG16 pg_dump to `tests/schema/expected.sql`, including schema_migration, ACLs, security labels and table access methods; only environment-specific ownership and non-executable comments are excluded, and two consecutive dumps must be byte-identical. Critical ownership/privilege separation is also asserted behaviorally. `psycopg2-binary==2.9.12` is approved as a pinned CI-only LGPL test dependency for the already-canonical referee and is never shipped in the application image. Rejected: a second Bun RLS oracle, direct psql schema loading, floating PostgreSQL tags, dump-vs-raw-migration text, ACL-blind drift, and an undeclared/unpinned Python install.
2026-08-15 · D-76 · Phase-0 completion text is reconciled before integration: D-14's NATS deferral overrides the stale Compose list; `SCHEMA.sql` is the historical source name while the immutable executable baseline is `migrations/0001_init.sql` and drift truth is the generated reviewed snapshot; Bun.password, built-in WS/SSE, and Bun.S3 remain binding zero-dependency choices but get executable coverage with their first real consumers, not empty Phase-0 smoke wrappers. Worktrees use Compose project isolation and configurable host ports rather than fixed container names. After Orders 008-013 are independently reviewed, one cumulative integration PR from the final head to main receives the full diff, full CI, 11/11 battery, and Claude review; lower stacked PRs close as superseded only after it merges. Rejected: propagating F6 through nine historical branches, merging intermediate branches into main, or declaring Phase 0 complete against knowingly stale DoD wording.
2026-08-15 · D-77 · D-73 transaction mechanism is narrowed by executable failure-path evidence: on Bun 1.3.14, successful `reserved.begin(callback)` stays on the reserved backend, but a rejected callback is still emitted as a fatal unhandled error and exits 1 even after the caller catches it. Migration and seed tooling therefore use explicit `BEGIN`/`COMMIT`/`ROLLBACK` on the reserved connection. A manual rollback probe stayed on one PID, left the connection usable, produced one controlled caught error, and exited 0. D-73's per-file atomicity, lock, checksum, and ledger decisions are unchanged. Rejected: treating a success-only connection-affinity probe as proof of failure semantics.
