2026-08-15 · D-98 · CORRECTS D-94: `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` on shared outbox rows is unsafe when multiple named consumers each require every event. PostgreSQL row locks are not namespaced by consumer: while consumer A locks seq 1, consumer B can skip seq 1, process seq 2, and advance its own cursor past the omitted event forever. `consumer_processed` cannot dedupe or recover an event never selected. Orders 022/023 instead insert then lock the per-consumer `consumer_cursor` row `FOR UPDATE`, read `outbox WHERE seq > last_seq ORDER BY seq LIMIT n` without row locks, and commit consumer effect + processed marker + cursor advance together. Same-name instances serialize; different names lock different cursor rows and each receive the complete stream. Order 022 gains an executable two-consumer concurrency proof; Order 023's multi-instance proof uses the same consumer name. Rejected: retaining SKIP LOCKED after cursor serialization as harmless decoration, because it still allows different names to steal rows; rejected: a new claims table or advisory locks when the cursor primary key already provides the correct lock namespace. Authorized under D-95; Claude must independently reproduce the concurrency proofs before integration.
